.el .ds Aq '
.TH tapectl-drive-cleaned 1  "tapectl-drive-cleaned " 
.SH NAME
tapectl\-drive\-cleaned \- Record that the drive was cleaned — a record, not an action
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBtapectl drive cleaned\fR [\fB\-\-cartridge\fR] [\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-\-device\fR] [\fB\-n\fR|\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR] [\fB\-v\fR|\fB\-\-verbose\fR] [\fB\-q\fR|\fB\-\-quiet\fR] [\fB\-y\fR|\fB\-\-yes\fR] [\fB\-\-config\fR] [\fB\-\-home\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] 
.SH DESCRIPTION
Record that the drive was cleaned — a record, not an action
.PP
tapectl drives no changer and never loads a cartridge, so cleaning is you inserting a cleaning cartridge when the drive asks for one (`drive status` shows "asks to be CLEANED"; `drive poll` reports the cleaning TapeAlert flag). This command writes down that it happened: one `cleaned` event on the drive, and, when a registered cleaning cartridge did it, one use of that cartridge. `report health` shows each drive\*(Aqs last cleaning and each cleaning cartridge\*(Aqs recorded uses against its rated life.
.PP
Which cleaning cartridge: `\-\-cartridge SERIAL`, or, without it, the one in the drive if its chip names a registered cleaning cartridge (read through the SCSI generic node; nothing moves, and nothing is sent while a tapectl command holds the drive). With neither, the cleaning is recorded against the drive alone and no cartridge\*(Aqs use is counted. Register a cleaning cartridge first with `drive cleaning\-cartridge add`.
.PP
The chip read that identifies the cartridge in the drive is kept whole in the MAM journal (`cartridge journal`), against no contact.
.PP
`\-\-dry\-run` reports what would be recorded and records nothing. With `\-\-json` the whole of stdout is one JSON object.
.PP
Exit status: 0 = recorded (a cartridge near its rated life is a warning with exit 0); 1 = recorded, and the cleaning cartridge is at or past its rated life (a spent cartridge does not clean; replace it); 2 = not recorded, the drive gave no serial to record against or the named cartridge is not registered.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-cartridge\fR \fI<CARTRIDGE>\fR
The cleaning cartridge used, by its registered serial

The serial it was registered under (`drive cleaning\-cartridge add`).
.TP
\fB\-\-device\fR \fI<DEVICE>\fR
The drive cleaned, by its tape node (default: the only one)

As configured (`[[backends.lto]].device_tape`; a by\-id link).
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.SH "GLOBAL OPTIONS"
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Output in JSON format, errors included

Under it an error is one JSON document on stdout, `{"error": …, "exit_code": N}`, and the exit code is the command\*(Aqs own.
.TP
\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
Show what would be done without making changes

Commands that cannot preview refuse the flag rather than ignore it.
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Debug tracing on stderr

Raises the log level to DEBUG (never lowers a `[logging] level` set higher); it never changes what a command does or prints on stdout.
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
No live progress on stderr

Long operations (stage create, volume write/resume/verify/read\-slices, restore) still write their session log under <home>/logs/.
.TP
\fB\-y\fR, \fB\-\-yes\fR
Answer yes to the confirmation prompts of risks you may accept

It never reaches a refusal: what is refused is a fact, not a risk to accept (ADR\-0008, Tier 3), and a command\*(Aqs own \-\-force names what it overrides.
.TP
\fB\-\-config\fR \fI<CONFIG>\fR
Path to config file

NOTE: on its own this ALSO relocates the whole tapectl home to the config file\*(Aqs parent directory — database, keys, staging, stage reports. That is how every test harness gets an isolated home, so it still works, but it is surprising enough that it now warns. Use \-\-home when you mean "operate on a different archive", and \-\-config only to point at a config file inside that home.
.TP
\fB\-\-home\fR \fI<HOME>\fR
tapectl home directory: database, keys, catalogs, stage\-reports, logs

The home is the first of: \-\-home; TAPECTL_HOME; the directory of \-\-config; this account\*(Aqs ~/.tapectl, when it exists; the home named by the home pointer /etc/tapectl/home (TAPECTL_HOME_POINTER names another file), when the account has no ~/.tapectl of its own; else ~/.tapectl, where `tapectl init` makes a new one. The config file is <home>/config.toml unless \-\-config names another. `tapectl status` and `tapectl config check` name the home in use and the rule that chose it.

As root the home pointer is never read, and on a host that has one tapectl refuses unless \-\-home or TAPECTL_HOME names the home: the service user\*(Aqs commands run through `tapectl\-op`.
